"Internal Error ()\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2925514\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2925514: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA review of Deborah Bird Rose’s ‘Reports from a Wild Country: Ethics for Decolonisation’ – AHR (https://australianhumanitiesreview.org/2007/08/01/a-review-of-deborah-bird-roses-reports-from-a-wild-country-ethics-for-decolonisation/)\nciteturn21view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://australianhumanitiesreview.org/2007/08/01/a-review-of-deborah-bird-roses-reports-from-a-wild-country-ethics-for-decolonisation/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 43\nL0: Close\nL1: \nL2: # A review of Deborah Bird Rose’s ‘Reports from a Wild Country: Ethics for Decolonisation’\nL3: ## by cite0†Val Plumwood L4: \nL5: © all rights reserved\nL6: \nL7: * * *\nL8: If our species does not survive the ecological crisis, it will probably be due to our failure to imagine and work out new ways to live with the earth, to rework ourselves and our high energy, high-consumption, and hyper-instrumental societies adaptively. We struggle to adjust because we’re still largely trapped inside the enlightenment tale of progress as human control over a passive and ‘dead’ nature that justifies both colonial conquests and commodity economies.\nL9: The real threat is not so much global warming itself, which there might still be a chance to head off, as our own inability to see past the post-enlightenment energy, control and consumption extravaganza we so naively identify with the good, civilised life to a sustainable form of human culture. The time of Homo reflectus , the self-critical and self-revising one , has surely come. Homo faber, the thoughtless tinkerer, is clearly not going to make it.\nL10: We will go onwards in a different mode of humanity, or not at all.\nL11: This is where studies of Indigenous culture can be so helpful, challenging the conceptual blockages that keep our minds closed to options for change. Reports from a Wild Country is a marvellous contribution to the key area of culture and sustainability, as a profound and important account of alternatives to western modernity that are highly relevant to our current plight.\nL12: Deborah Bird Rose, long-time student and friend of the Yarralin people of the Victoria River country, outlines a project that is dialogical and recuperative, humble, one that ‘seeks glimpses of illumination, and aims towards engagement and disclosure’.\nL13: Nevertheless, Rose’s sophisticated and skilful philosophical analysis of conceptual frameworks of time, country, life and death shows that Indigenous concepts of human identity allow forms of ecological respect, restraint and recognition the dominant culture has lost sight of or never achieved.\nL14: There are those who deny that Indigenous culture has anything of value to tell us about ecological adaptation, while others can only see learning in terms of ‘stealing’ Indigenous power, ideas or identity. Rose steers a helpful and well-judged course between various such hazards that threaten the project. She notes that settlers can be attracted to Indigenous ideas for instrumental reasons that subtly continue the pattern of exploitation.\nL15: But Rose does not follow the fashion for dismissing all desire to learn from Indigenous culture as ‘appropriation’, a mere cynical ploy designed to allow settlers to avoid acknowledging the wrongs we have done. The reader is never spared the hard knowledge of those wrongs, that settlers are here in this land through dispossession and death, ‘Settler societies are brought into being through invasion: death and silence pervade and gird the whole project’ (p. 58).\nL16: But this hard knowledge is balanced with the promise that the respectful seeker can learn much, not least about our own sources of violence, indifference and endless deferral to the future. The ground itself, Rose says, holds traces not only of our damage but also of better possibilities (p. 8).\nL17: The opening chapters of the book’s first section establish the philosophical framework, and the main themes — resilience, violence, counter-modernity, time and death. These themes are discussed in the light of an ethics of connection based on feminist thought and on the work of Levinas (p. 24), in which life with others is inherently tangled in responsibility, and indifference to or justification of the suffering of others is at the root of all immorality.\nL18: Rose adds ‘Our Australian context presses us to consider not only the justification of others’ pain but the denial of it as well’ (p. 14)\nL19: A fascinating chapter illuminates the master western narrative of progress naturalised in our concepts of time and economy, what Bauman calls the calculus of progress, in which ‘present distress can be claimed to be leading towards, and be justified by, a more perfect future’. The pervasively future-orientated societies of the west define an ontological break that determines that the past is finished (p. 17).\nL20: For example, the major ontological disjunctive moment for Christians consists of the birth, death and resurrection of Christ, transfiguring the conditions of life on earth for believers. Rose’s argument is that western colonising practice ‘replicates this pattern as the foundational template for frontier time, space and action’ (p. 59) – Year Zero — a framework that discounts those who came before and fosters ingratitude and delusions of disconnection.\nL21: Indigenous society, by contrast, has a basic orientation towards origins (p. 55) rather than towards a future state. The implications of this are profound. Far from the past, the Dreaming, being finished, ‘its action continues in the present in the bodies of all living things whose origins are in the Dreaming’, (p. 56) while Dreaming action continues in the present through ceremony, creation, song and other forms of creative memory and connection.\nL22: ‘Memory, place, dead bodies and genealogies hold the stories that tell the histories that are not erased, and that refuse erasure. Painful as they are, they also constitute relationships of moral responsibility, binding people into the country and the generations of their lives.’ (p. 57)\nL23: It is in their thinking about death and life that we find perhaps the greatest philosophical achievements of these Indigenous cultures. The Western problematic of death – where the essential self is disembodied spirit — poses a false choice of continuity, even eternity, in the realm of the spirit, versus the reductive materialist concept of death as the complete ending of the story of the material, embodied self.\nL24: Both horns of this dilemma exact a terrible price, alienation from the earth in the first case and the loss of meaning in the second. Indigenous concepts of self and death succeed in breaking this pernicious false choice and suggesting satisfying and ecologically responsive forms of continuity with the earth. By understanding life as in circulation, as a gift from a community of ancestors, we can see death as recycling, a flowing on into an ecological and ancestral community of origins.\nL25: In place of the western war of life against death whose battleground has been variously the spirit-identified afterlife and the reduced, medicalised material life, the Indigenous imaginary sees death as part of life, partly through narrative, and partly because death is a return to the (highly narrativised) land that nurtures life.\nL26: Such a vision of death fosters an imaginary of the land as a ‘nourishing terrain’cite1†^{1} , and of death as a nurturing, material continuity with ecological others, especially the lives and landforms of country. We grasp these solutions and glimpse their transformative power, only to learn in the last section that they are under siege from western-based organisations and religions that are working – still! — to undermine such worldviews and impose their own maladaptive ones.\nL27: Much of this sad conflict between ‘church way’ and ‘culture way’ people and interpretations is taking place precisely around these concepts and practices of human identity, death and country, concepts the west has interpreted in alienated ways hostile to the earth. But through this inspiring book the original Yarralin vision may yet contribute to finding a better way for our species.\nL28: \nL29: \nL30: Val Plumwood\nL31: Fenner School of Environment and Society\nL32: Australian National University\nL33: \nL34: Reports from a Wild Country: Ethics for Decolonisation was published in Sydney by UNSW Press in 2004 ( 256pp, ISBN 9780868407982, PB\nL35: $39.95)\nL36: \nL37: FOOTNOTES:\nL38: \nL39: cite2†1. See Deborah Bird Rose Nourishing Terrains , (Canberra, Australian Heritage Commission, 1996).\nL40: \nL41: In the Australian Humanities Review, see also: cite3†Ecological Humanities , cite4†Issue 42, August 2007 , cite5†Plumwood, Val , cite6†Reviews L42: If you would like to contribute to this discussion, please email cite7†ahr@anu.edu.au --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - Watts Final.docx (https://ikdll.nau.edu/id/eprint/80/1/titusland%2C%2BWatts%2BFinal.pdf)\nciteturn21view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn19view1\",\"id\":9}); Redirected to URL: https://ikdll.nau.edu/id/eprint/80/1/titusland,%2BWatts%2BFinal.pdf; Total lines: 610\nL0@P0: URL: https://ikdll.nau.edu/id/eprint/80/1/titusland,%2BWatts%2BFinal.pdf\nL1@P0: Decolonization: Indigeneity, Education & Society\nL2@P0: Vol. 2, No. 1, 2013, pp. 20-34\nL3@P0: 2013 V. Watts This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution\nL4@P0: Noncommercial 3.0 Unported License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0), permitting all non-commercial use,\nL5@P0: distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\nL6@P0: Indigenous place-thought & agency\nL7@P0: amongst humans and non-humans (First\nL8@P0: Woman and Sky Woman go on a\nL9@P0: European world tour!)\nL10@P0: Vanessa Watts\nL11@P0: Queen’s University, Canada\nL12@P0: Abstract\nL13@P0: This article will examine how agency is circulated through human and non-human worlds in the\nL14@P0: creation and maintenance of society from an Indigenous point of view. Through processes of\nL15@P0: colonization, the corruption of essential categories of Indigenous conceptions of the world (the\nL16@P0: feminine and land) has led to a disconnect between how this agency is manifested in Indigenous\nL17@P0: societies. Through a comparison between the epistemological-ontological divide and an\nL18@P0: Indigenous conception of Place-Thought, this article will argue that agency has erroneously\nL19@P0: become exclusive to humans, thereby removing non-human agency from what constitutes a\nL20@P0: society. This is accomplished in part by mythologizing Indigenous origin stories and separating\nL21@P0: out communication, treaty-making, and historical agreements that human beings held with the\nL22@P0: animal world, the sky world, the spirit world, etc. In order for colonialism to operationalize itself,\nL23@P0: it must attempt to make Indigenous peoples stand in disbelief of themselves and their histories.\nL24@P0: This article attempts to reaffirm this sacred connection between place, non-human and human in\nL25@P0: an effort to access the “pre-colonial mind”.\nL26@P0-1: Keywords: agency; epistemology; ontology; land; Indigenous womenIndigenous place-thought and agency 21\nL27@P1: Introduction\nL28@P1: According to Haudenosaunee, Sky Woman fell from a hole in the sky. John Mohawk (2005)\nL29@P1: writes of her journey towards the waters below. On her descent, Sky Woman fell through the\nL30@P1: clouds and air towards water below. During her descent, birds could see this falling creature and\nL31@P1: saw she could not fly. They came to her and helped to lower her slowly to waters beneath her.\nL32@P1: The birds told Turtle that she must need a place to land, as she possessed no water legs. Turtle\nL33@P1: rose up, breaking through the surface so that Sky Woman could land on Turtle’s back. Once\nL34@P1: landed, Sky Woman and Turtle began to form the earth, the land becoming an extension of their\nL35@P1: bodies.\nL36@P1: Amongst the Anishnaabe, a similar history is shared. Leanne Simpson (2011) retells the\nL37@P1: Anishnaabe Creation Story, within the historical framework of the Seven Fires of Creation. The\nL38@P1: two fires that I would like to relate to this idea of Place-Thought, is the Fifth and Sixth Fire. In\nL39@P1: the Fifth Fire, Gizhe-Mnidoo (the Creator) placed his/her thoughts into seeds. In the Sixth Fire,\nL40@P1: Gizhe-Mnidoo created First Woman (Earth), a place where these seeds could root and grow.\nL41@P1: Before continuing, I would like to emphasize that these two events took place. They were\nL42@P1: not imagined or fantasized. This is not lore, myth or legend. These histories are not longer\nL43@P1: versions of “and the moral of the story is….”. This is what happened.\nL44@P1: These Creation histories can sometimes take days to describe. For the purposes of this\nL45@P1: article, I would like to focus on a common historical understanding of the origin of the human\nL46@P1: species - the spiritual and the feminine. These historical accounts, two of many, speak to the\nL47@P1: common intersections of the female, animals, the spirit world, and the mineral and plant world.\nL48@P1: What constitutes “society” from these perspectives revolves around interactions between these\nL49@P1: worlds rather than solely interactions amongst human beings. Both of these accounts describe a\nL50@P1: theoretical understanding of the world via a physical embodiment – Place-Thought. Place\u0002Thought is the non-distinctive space where place and thought were never separated because they\nL51@P1: never could or can be separated. Place-Thought is based upon the premise that land is alive and\nL52@P1: thinking and that humans and non-humans derive agency through the extensions of these\nL53@P1: thoughts.\nL54@P1: Given this, Indigenous perceptions of whom and what contributes to a societal structure\nL55@P1: are quite different from traditional Euro-Western thought. The evaluation of human interaction\nL56@P1: and culture has been a concern of traditional sociology since its inception and has led to the\nL57@P1: definition of what constitutes a society or various societies. The idea of “society” has revolved\nL58@P1: around human beings and their special place in the world, given their capacity for reason and\nL59@P1: language. Though this idea of society is still largely attributed to human relationships, in recent\nL60@P1: times we can see the emergence of non-humans being evaluated in terms of their contributions to\nL61@P1: the development and maintenance of society.\nL62@P1: This article will examine how agency circulates inside of two different frames: Place\u0002Thought (Haudenosaunee and Anishnaabe cosmologies) and epistemological-ontological (Euro\u0002Western frame). My intention is both to emphasize a differentiated framing of Indigenous\nL63@P1-2: cosmologies as well as to examine our rich and intelligent theories found in these cosmologies.22 V. Watts\nL64@P2: \nL65@P2: As an Anishnaabe and Haudenosaunee woman, my worldview is continuously tested\nL66@P2: against the colonial frame. It is therefore an important task to imagine and strive for our original\nL67@P2: instructions, which are located in what Susan Hill (2011) describes as the “pre-colonial\nL68@P2: mind”. Given this constant conflict, it is necessary to tease out what the land’s intentions might\nL69@P2: be, and how she tries to speak through us.\nL70@P2: Reclaiming\nL71@P2:   our\nL72@P2:   frame\nL73@P2: Our understandings of the world are often viewed as mythic by “modern” society, while our\nL74@P2: stories are considered to be an alternative mode of understanding and interpretation rather than\nL75@P2: “real” events. Colonization is not solely an attack on peoples and lands; rather, this attack is\nL76@P2: accomplished in part through purposeful and ignorant misrepresentations of Indigenous\nL77@P2: cosmologies. In order to demonstrate this, let us examine how First Woman (Earth) and Sky\nL78@P2: Woman are translated through a foreign epistemological-ontological divide.\nL79@P2: Frameworks are designs of understanding and interpretation. They are the basis for how\nL80@P2: humans organize politically, philosophically, etc. Frameworks in a Euro-Western sense exist in\nL81@P2: the abstract. How they are articulated in action or behavior brings this abstraction into praxis;\nL82@P2: hence a division of epistemological/theoretical versus ontological/praxis. The difference in a\nL83@P2: Haudenosaunee or Anishnaabe framework is that our cosmological frameworks are not an\nL84@P2: abstraction but rather a literal and animate extension of Sky Woman’s and First Woman’s\nL85@P2: thoughts; it is impossible to separate theory from praxis if we believe in the original historical\nL86@P2: events of Sky Woman and First Woman. So it is not that Indigenous peoples do not theorize, but\nL87@P2: that these complex theories are not distinct from place.\nL88@P2: Below is a visual representation of these two separate framings. On the left is a depiction\nL89@P2: of how an Anishnaabe and/or Haudenosaunee cosmology might be represented. On the right, the\nL90@P2: process by which a Euro-Western meta-understanding can contribute to colonization of these\nL91@P2-3: Indigenous cosmologies:Indigenous place-thought and agency 23\nL92@P3: The above figure is not intended to be a universal model of how all Indigenous peoples\nL93@P3: think, believe, etc. Rather, it is simply a depiction of the crucial differences between Indigenous\nL94@P3: and Euro-Western processes. The visual on the left describes the animate nature of land. To be\nL95@P3: animate goes beyond being alive or acting, it is to be full of thought, desire, contemplation and\nL96@P3: will. It is the literal embodiment of the feminine, of First Woman, by which many Indigenous\nL97@P3: origin stories find their inception. When Sky Woman falls from the sky and lies on the back of a\nL98@P3: turtle, she is not only able to create land but becomes territory itself. Therefore, Place-Thought is\nL99@P3: an extension of her circumstance, desire, and communication with the water and animals – her\nL100@P3: agency. Through this communication she is able to become the basis by which all future societies\nL101@P3: will be built upon – land.\nL102@P3: In becoming land or territory, she becomes the designator of how living beings will\nL103@P3: organize upon her. Where waters flow and pool, where mountains rise and turn into valleys, all\nL104@P3: of these become demarcations of who will reside where, how they will live, and how their\nL105@P3: behaviours toward one another are determined. Scientists refer to this as ecosystems or habitats.\nL106@P3: However, if we accept the idea that all living things contain spirit, then this extends beyond\nL107@P3: complex structures within an ecosystem. It means that non-human beings choose how they\nL108@P3: reside, interact and develop relationships with other non-humans. So, all elements of nature\nL109@P3: possess agency, and this agency is not limited to innate action or causal relationships.\nL110@P3: Thus, habitats and ecosystems are better understood as societies from an Indigenous point\nL111@P3: of view; meaning that they have ethical structures, inter-species treaties and agreements, and\nL112@P3: further their ability to interpret, understand and implement. Non-human beings are active\nL113@P3: members of society. Not only are they active, they also directly influence how humans organize\nL114@P3: themselves into that society. The very existence of clan systems evidences these many historical\nL115@P3: agreements between humans and non-humans. Clan systems vary from community to\nL116@P3: community and are largely dependent on the surrounding landscape. For example, whale clans\nL117@P3: are not present amongst Indigenous nations where there is no access to seawater. The structure of\nL118@P3: societies is demarcated by territory, which again, is an extension of Sky Woman’s original\nL119@P3: circumstance. She is present in the relationships between humans and humans, humans and non\u0002humans, and non-humans and non-humans.\nL120@P3: Human thought and action are therefore derived from a literal expression of particular\nL121@P3: places and historical events in Haudenosaunee and Anishnaabe cosmologies. The agency that\nL122@P3: place possesses can be thought of in a similar way that Western thinkers locate agency in human\nL123@P3: beings. It follows that if, as Indigenous peoples, we are extensions of the very land we walk\nL124@P3: upon, than we have an obligation to maintain communication with it. A familiar warning is\nL125@P3: echoed through many communities, that if we do not care for the land we run the risk of losing\nL126@P3: who we are as Indigenous peoples. When this warning is examined in terms of original Place\u0002Thought, it is not only the threat of a lost identity or physical displacement that is risked but our\nL127@P3: ability to think, act, and govern becomes compromised because this relationship is continuously\nL128@P3: corrupted with foreign impositions of how agency is organized. Colonization has disrupted our\nL129@P3-4: ability to communicate with place and has endangered agency amongst Indigenous peoples. The 24 V. Watts\nL130@P4: \nL131@P4: pre-colonial mind was confronted with a form of diminutive agency, and the process by which\nL132@P4: we ensured our own ability to act and converse with non-humans and other humans became\nL133@P4: compromised. A disruption of this original process goes beyond losing a form of Indigenous\nL134@P4: identity or worldview and how it is practiced – it has become a violation of Sky Woman’s\nL135@P4: intentionality.\nL136@P4: The epistemological-ontological divide processes agency much differently. A common\nL137@P4: understanding of epistemology would describe it as one’s perception of the world as being\nL138@P4: distinct from what is in the world, or what constitutes it (Descartes, 1996). Thought and ideas are\nL139@P4: reserved for the one perceiving – humans. All other objects, actants, or beings in the world may\nL140@P4: have an essence (Kant, 1999; Latour, 1987) or an interconnection with humans, but their ability\nL141@P4: to perceive is null or limited to instinctual reactions.\nL142@P4: The epistemological-ontological removes the how and why out of the what. The what is\nL143@P4: left empty, readied for inscription. Epistemology has many representations: there is Science,\nL144@P4: Christianity, Eurocentrism, Marxism, communism, etc. Ontology too contains many variables:\nL145@P4: do objects have an essence? What is in the world and how do its parts formulate a society? All of\nL146@P4: these concerns are by their very nature pursuits of human quandary and based on a capacity for\nL147@P4: reason. These distinct domains provide evidence that humans are assumed to be separate from\nL148@P4: the world they are in, in order to have a perception of it1 (Kant, 2008). This is one theoretical\nL149@P4: structure to understand the world and its constituents. It necessitates a separation of not only\nL150@P4: human and non-human, but a hierarchy of beings in terms of how beings are able to think as\nL151@P4: well.\nL152@P4: The man-made distinction between what and how/why is not an innocent one. Its\nL153@P4: consequences can be disastrous for not only non-humans but humans as well. If we lay this\nL154@P4: framing atop of nature, humankind is elevated outside or above the natural world. The reasoning\nL155@P4: being that perception is a gift or trait bestowed to the human mind, and most certainly not\nL156@P4: something possessed by a stone or a river. A river may act (i.e. flow) but does it perceive or\nL157@P4: contemplate this? An Anishnaabe perspective would respond in the affirmative. As we can see\nL158@P4: from the process of colonization and the imposition of the epistemology-ontology frame, our\nL159@P4: communication and obligations with other beings of creation is continuously interrupted.\nL160@P4: For example, in the Christian origin story, we see how the interaction between a female\nL161@P4: (Eve) and non-humans (Serpent, Tree of Knowledge, apple) led to the damnation of all future\nL162@P4: humankind (Oh, that nosy woman!). It also meant that the garden, in which they were able to\nL163@P4: reside, quickly became a place where humans were cast out. They were no longer of their\nL164@P4: surroundings, but outside of them. The result has many consequences, but two significant ones\nL165@P4: for this purpose emerged.\nL166@P4: Firstly, humans were positioned into a world in which they were able to reside over\nL167@P4: nature. Secondly, and interdependently, humans resolved that communication with nature held\nL168@P4: disastrous effects (Tree of Knowledge, the Serpent) and so inter-species communication became\nL169@P4: \nL170@P4: \nL171@P4: \nL172@P4: \nL173@P4: \nL174@P4: \nL175@P4: \nL176@P4: \nL177@P4: \nL178@P4: \nL179@P4: \nL180@P4: \nL181@P4: \nL182@P4: \nL183@P4: \nL184@P4: \nL185@P4: \nL186@P4: \nL187@P4: \nL188@P4: \nL189@P4: \nL190@P4: \nL191@P4: \nL192@P4: \nL193@P4: \nL194@P4: \nL195@P4: \nL196@P4: \nL197@P4: \nL198@P4: \nL199@P4: \nL200@P4: \nL201@P4: \nL202@P4: \nL203@P4: \nL204@P4: \nL205@P4: \nL206@P4: \nL207@P4: \nL208@P4: \nL209@P4: \nL210@P4: \nL211@P4: \nL212@P4: \nL213@P4: \nL214@P4: \nL215@P4: \nL216@P4: \nL217@P4: \nL218@P4: \nL219@P4: \nL220@P4: \nL221@P4: \nL222@P4: \nL223@P4: \nL224@P4: \nL225@P4: \nL226@P4: \nL227@P4: \nL228@P4: \nL229@P4:    1 For more information, please refer to the subject-object argument from Immanuel Kant’s Copernican\nL230@P4-5: Revolution in Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason.Indigenous place-thought and agency 25\nL231@P5: quite limited if not profane. In this worldview, agency became associated with human-human\nL232@P5: interactions. Societies were built upon domination over nature because of a perception that\nL233@P5: human arrangements with the animal world were unnecessary, if not dangerous. From an\nL234@P5: Indigenous perspective, though, the same interaction between a female and non-human has\nL235@P5: different results. For many Indigenous peoples, being aligned with the animal world was a\nL236@P5: position that was treated with respect and honour (Sioui, 1992). This relational paradox created a\nL237@P5: point of devastation, where our most sacred elements (land and women) were violently corrupted\nL238@P5: with a false profanity.\nL239@P5: Conversely, in many Indigenous origin stories the idea that humans were the last species\nL240@P5: to arrive on earth was central; it also meant that humans arrived in a state of dependence on an\nL241@P5: already-functioning society with particular values, ethics, etc. (Benton-Benai, 2010). The\nL242@P5: inclusion of humans into this society meant that certain agreements, arrangements, etc. had to be\nL243@P5: made with the animal world, plant world, sky world, mineral world and other non-human\nL244@P5: species. Therefore, being associated with animals, whether it be through clan systems,\nL245@P5: ceremonies, or beings that acted as advisors, transpired from a place of reverence. In the\nL246@P5: Haudenosaunee origin story, Sky Woman becomes curious and falls through a hole in the sky\nL247@P5: and she is safely brought down to earth by different birds who land her on the back of a\nL248@P5: turtle. With the help of other animals, they are able to create territory, and the beginning of\nL249@P5: humankind.\nL250@P5: Both the story of Genesis and the story of Sky Woman tell of a world that existed before\nL251@P5: humans. Both tell of a woman and non-humans interacting to create significant changes in\nL252@P5: creation as well as for humankind. In the latter, the relationship between animals and this female\nL253@P5: is regarded as sacred and ritualized over generations. This relationship also becomes the\nL254@P5: foundation for future clan systems, ethics, governance, ceremonies, etc. In the former, the female\nL255@P5: becomes responsible for all the pain of childbirth and resentment for being cast out of\nL256@P5: paradise. The interaction of Eve and the Serpent results in shame and excommunication from\nL257@P5: nature. Additionally, future dialogue and communication with animals becomes taboo and a\nL258@P5: source of witchcraft. It is at this point of conflict where thought, perception, and action are\nL259@P5: separated from the supposed inertia of nature.\nL260@P5: Governance,\nL261@P5:   agency,\nL262@P5:   and\nL263@P5:   non-­‐humans\nL264@P5: If we begin from the premise that land is female and further, that she thinks - then she is alive. If\nL265@P5: the most elemental female is conceived of as being responsible for pain, shame and\nL266@P5: excommunication, then doing destruction upon her does not seem that bad. In fact, maybe she\nL267@P5: even deserves to have violence done to her. After all, her curious nature compromised the life\nL268@P5: human beings could have had, but cannot experience because of her irresponsible actions – thus\nL269@P5: the basis for resentment. Any obligation to be empathic towards her is no longer necessary\nL270@P5: because this dominant worldview instructs that the feminine is synonymous with disappointment\nL271@P5-6: and stupidity. It is no surprise then, that amidst a Euro-Christian construct, land and its 26 V. Watts\nL272@P6: \nL273@P6: designations are silenced. Many Indigenous peoples wonder at how much destruction has\nL274@P6: persisted throughout the decades by the colonizer without any significant attempt at stopping. If\nL275@P6: you belong to a structure where land and the feminine are not only less-than, but knowingly\nL276@P6: irresponsible, violations against her would seem warranted.\nL277@P6: When thinking about agency with reference to Place-Thought, where can it be located? I\nL278@P6: find it in animals, in humans, in plants, in rocks, etc. How did I come to think that these different\nL279@P6: entities and beings had agency in the first place? From stories/histories. For example, an event\nL280@P6: took place, perhaps, between a bear and a young woman and from this meeting an idea about a\nL281@P6: clan system came to be. Or maybe Three Sisters, named Corn, Bean and Squash decided to make\nL282@P6: an arrangement about how they would live together. Maybe it seems like I am telling stories but\nL283@P6: really I am commenting on two examples of historical events that took place in a particular\nL284@P6: location, at a particular time, where consciousness, thought, desire, and the imagination of all\nL285@P6: individuals is in action.\nL286@P6: In an epistemological-ontological frame, Indigenous cosmologies would be examples of a\nL287@P6: symbolic interconnectedness – an abstraction of a moral code. It would be a way in which to\nL288@P6: view the world – the basis for an epistemological stance. From a Haudenosaunee worldview, this\nL289@P6: is what happened. Further, Haudenosaunee systems, peoples, territories, etc. are affected by this\nL290@P6: relationship between the Three Sisters. It is more than a lesson, a teaching, or even an historical\nL291@P6: account. Their conscious and knowing agreement directly extends to our philosophies, thoughts\nL292@P6: and actions as Haudenosaunee peoples.\n"
